.TH CRAB 1 "2026-09-08" "crabbucket 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
crab \- build a crabbucket site
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B crab
.I COMMAND
.RI [ OPTION ]...
.RI [ DIRECTORY ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B crab
builds a static site from Markdown content and a
.B site.toml
into a directory of plain HTML, CSS and, if asked for, about a kilobyte of
JavaScript.
.PP
The build is a gate rather than a formatter.  It refuses to finish when a page
has no title, when a page names a layout the design system does not have, or
when any page links to a route, an asset or a heading that does not exist.  A
build that succeeds has produced a site whose internal links all work.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BI build " [DIRECTORY]"
Build the site in
.I DIRECTORY
(default: the current directory).  Output goes to
.IR DIRECTORY /dist
unless
.B \-\-out
says otherwise.
.TP
.BI new " DIRECTORY"
Scaffold a new site in
.IR DIRECTORY :
a configuration file, three pages, a
.B turborust.toml
for the dev loop, and a GitHub Pages workflow.  The result builds with no
edits.
.TP
.BR dev ", " serve
Print where the dev server went, and exit 2.  There is no built-in dev server;
see
.B THE DEV LOOP
below.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS For build
.TP
.BI \-\-out " PATH"
Write the site to
.I PATH
instead of
.IR dist/ .
.TP
.BI \-\-base " PATH"
Serve from
.I PATH
instead of the base recorded in
.BR site.toml .
Every spelling normalises identically, so
.IR repo ", " /repo ", " repo/ " and " /repo/
all mean the same thing.
.SS For new
.TP
.BI \-\-base " PATH"
The path the scaffolded site will be served from.  Defaults to
.IR /NAME/ ,
because a GitHub Pages project site is the common case.
.TP
.B \-\-router
Ship the client-side router.
.TP
.B \-\-force
Scaffold into a directory that is not empty.
.SS General
.TP
.B \-\-help
Display a usage summary and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Output version information and exit.
.PP
A bare
.B \-\-
ends option processing, so a directory whose name begins with a dash is still
reachable.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The site was built.
.TP
.B 1
The site is wrong: a page failed to parse, or a link goes nowhere.
.TP
.B 2
The command line is wrong.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B site.toml
The site's configuration: its title, description, absolute
.IR url ,
the
.I base
path it is served from, and whether to ship the
.IR router .
.TP
.B content/
Markdown pages with
.B +++
TOML frontmatter.  A file's path below this directory is its route, and any
.B index.md
takes the route of its directory.
.TP
.B content/404.md
Optional.  Written as
.BR 404.html ,
which is what a static host serves in place of a missing path.  Because it is
served from every path, a relative link on it fails the build.
.TP
.B static/
Copied into the output verbatim.
.TP
.B dist/
The built site.  Safe to delete; never edit.
.SH THE DEV LOOP
There is no
.BR "crab dev" ,
and there will not be one.
.B turborust
already supervises the build, derives its watch globs from the crate
dependency closure, serves the output with live reload, and shows build errors
in a browser overlay.  Run:
.PP
.RS
.B turborust up
.RE
.PP
.B crab new
writes a
.B turborust.toml
that does this.
.SH EXAMPLES
Build the site in the current directory:
.PP
.RS
.B crab build
.RE
.PP
Scaffold a site for a GitHub Pages project site, with the client router:
.PP
.RS
.B crab new my\-project \-\-router
.RE
.PP
Build a preview served from a different path, without editing the
configuration:
.PP
.RS
.B crab build \-\-out /tmp/preview \-\-base /preview/
.RE
